1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:04,437
28 June 1914.

2
00:00:05,037 --> 00:00:09,648
In Sarajevo, a car carrying the heir
to an empire takes a wrong turn,

3
00:00:09,798 --> 00:00:13,038
and stops near a
19-year-old with a pistol.

4
00:00:13,438 --> 00:00:14,311
Two shots.

5
00:00:14,911 --> 00:00:18,042
Within six weeks, Europe's
great powers are at war.

6
00:00:18,342 --> 00:00:21,037
Four years later, around 9 to 10 million

7
00:00:21,037 --> 00:00:24,270
soldiers are dead, and
four empires are falling.

8
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:26,120
How did it happen?

9
00:00:26,370 --> 00:00:27,718
And how did it end?

10
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,548
To understand how, we have to look at
Europe before the shooting started.

11
00:00:40,868 --> 00:00:44,460
It was a continent of great
powers and rival empires,

12
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:48,256
competing for land, trade and
colonies around the world.

13
00:00:48,576 --> 00:00:51,704
Over the years, they had
drifted into two camps.

14
00:00:52,024 --> 00:00:55,200
On one side stood France
and Russia, with Britain

15
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,182
more loosely tied to
them: the Triple Entente.

16
00:00:58,502 --> 00:01:03,243
On the other, Germany and Austria-Hungary,
partners with Italy in the

17
00:01:03,243 --> 00:01:07,778
Triple Alliance, although Italy
would stay out of the war in 1914.

18
00:01:08,098 --> 00:01:11,248
The powers built ever-larger
armies, and Britain

19
00:01:11,248 --> 00:01:14,202
and Germany were locked
in a naval arms race.

20
00:01:14,522 --> 00:01:17,777
In the Balkans, nationalism
was on the rise.

21
00:01:18,097 --> 00:01:21,173
Austria-Hungary, an
empire of many peoples,

22
00:01:21,173 --> 00:01:24,106
saw Serb nationalism as a serious threat.

23
00:01:24,426 --> 00:01:28,729
And the war plans depended on
speed. Germany's plan was to knock

24
00:01:28,729 --> 00:01:33,032
France out fast, through Belgium,
then turn east against Russia.

25
00:01:33,532 --> 00:01:36,100
None of this made war inevitable.

26
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:40,371
But it meant that a local crisis
could spread very quickly.

27
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:56,409
On 28 June 1914, Archduke Franz
Ferdinand, heir to Austria-Hungary's

28
00:01:56,409 --> 00:02:01,523
throne, and his wife Sophie were
visiting Sarajevo, in Bosnia.

29
00:02:01,843 --> 00:02:06,055
After a wrong turn, the car
stopped near Gavrilo Princip,

30
00:02:06,055 --> 00:02:09,824
a 19-year-old Bosnian Serb.
He shot them both dead.

31
00:02:10,324 --> 00:02:13,436
In early July, Germany
promised Austria-Hungary

32
00:02:13,436 --> 00:02:16,548
its full backing: the
so-called "blank cheque".

33
00:02:16,868 --> 00:02:23,334
On 23 July, Vienna sent Serbia an
ultimatum, with 48 hours to reply.

34
00:02:23,654 --> 00:02:26,948
Serbia accepted most of
the demands, but not all.

35
00:02:27,348 --> 00:02:31,160
On 28 July, Austria-Hungary declared war.

36
00:02:31,480 --> 00:02:35,719
Russia began to mobilise.
Germany declared war on Russia

37
00:02:35,719 --> 00:02:39,655
and France, and on 4 August
invaded neutral Belgium.

38
00:02:39,975 --> 00:02:43,488
Britain had guaranteed
Belgium's neutrality,

39
00:02:43,488 --> 00:02:47,241
and at 11 p.m. London
time, Britain was at war.

40
00:02:47,741 --> 00:02:50,932
Sarajevo was the trigger,
not the whole cause.

41
00:02:51,252 --> 00:02:54,725
Historians still argue
about who was most to blame.

42
00:02:55,045 --> 00:02:59,404
Some put the main responsibility
on Germany and Austria-Hungary.

43
00:02:59,404 --> 00:03:03,286
Others stress that many
governments, in a tangled system,

44
00:03:03,286 --> 00:03:07,304
made choices that led to a
world war none of them intended.

45
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,101
Many on all sides believed
the war would be short.

46
00:03:14,501 --> 00:03:18,601
In August, German armies swept
through Belgium and into France.

47
00:03:18,921 --> 00:03:23,124
The British fought their first major
battle at Mons, then retreated.

48
00:03:23,444 --> 00:03:27,160
Then, in early September, French
and British troops stopped

49
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:31,001
the German advance east of Paris,
at the Battle of the Marne.

50
00:03:31,321 --> 00:03:35,130
Germany's hopes of a quick
victory in the west were over.

51
00:03:35,530 --> 00:03:38,382
Both sides then raced
north, trying to outflank

52
00:03:38,382 --> 00:03:40,749
each other, until they reached the sea.

53
00:03:41,069 --> 00:03:45,114
By the end of 1914, a line
of trenches ran for about

54
00:03:45,114 --> 00:03:49,315
700 kilometres, from the
Belgian coast to Switzerland.

55
00:03:49,815 --> 00:03:52,641
And at Christmas, something
remarkable happened.

56
00:03:52,961 --> 00:03:57,581
Along parts of the front, men stopped
fighting. They sang carols, swapped

57
00:03:57,581 --> 00:04:02,455
souvenirs and buried their dead, and in
some places they kicked a ball about.

58
00:04:02,775 --> 00:04:07,885
Rifleman William Eve wrote in his diary:
"not a shot fired all day or night."

59
00:04:08,385 --> 00:04:12,081
But the truces were unofficial
and patchy, senior officers

60
00:04:12,081 --> 00:04:15,585
disapproved, and it never
happened on that scale again.

61
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:23,715
For the men at the front,
trench life was grim.

62
00:04:24,035 --> 00:04:27,038
Units rotated in and
out of the front line,

63
00:04:27,038 --> 00:04:30,110
usually spending a few
days there at a time.

64
00:04:30,430 --> 00:04:35,401
Lice infested their clothes and spread
trench fever. Rats were everywhere.

65
00:04:35,721 --> 00:04:40,439
After long hours in cold water, feet
could swell and rot: trench foot.

66
00:04:40,759 --> 00:04:43,626
Shellfire, not bullets,
was the great killer.

67
00:04:44,026 --> 00:04:50,628
In April 1915, near Ypres, poison gas was
used on a large scale for the first time.

68
00:04:50,948 --> 00:04:55,034
Both sides soon issued masks.
Gas caused many casualties but

69
00:04:55,034 --> 00:04:58,915
relatively few deaths: it was
feared more than it killed.

70
00:04:59,315 --> 00:05:03,544
The strain broke many minds.
The British Army alone recorded

71
00:05:03,544 --> 00:05:08,125
about 80,000 cases of shell shock,
and many more went unrecorded.

72
00:05:08,445 --> 00:05:13,387
In all, 306 British and
Commonwealth soldiers were executed,

73
00:05:13,387 --> 00:05:18,577
mostly for desertion or cowardice.
In 2006, they were pardoned.

74
00:05:20,800 --> 00:05:22,405
This was a world war.

75
00:05:22,725 --> 00:05:28,650
In the east, in August 1914, Germany
destroyed a Russian army at Tannenberg.

76
00:05:28,970 --> 00:05:33,565
In April 1915, Allied troops,
including the Anzacs, landed

77
00:05:33,565 --> 00:05:38,001
at Gallipoli, in the Ottoman
Empire, Germany's new ally.

78
00:05:38,321 --> 00:05:41,582
The campaign failed, at
a heavy cost in lives.

79
00:05:41,902 --> 00:05:46,570
Italy joined the Allies, and fighting
spread to Africa and the Middle East.

80
00:05:46,890 --> 00:05:51,856
About one and a half million men from
South Asia served in the Indian Army,

81
00:05:51,856 --> 00:05:56,491
and troops came from across the
empires, from Canada to the Caribbean.

82
00:05:57,191 --> 00:06:01,195
Inside the Ottoman Empire,
a catastrophe unfolded.

83
00:06:01,595 --> 00:06:07,789
From 1915, the Ottoman government deported
and murdered Armenians across the empire.

84
00:06:08,109 --> 00:06:12,816
An estimated 600,000 to 1.5
million Armenians died, in

85
00:06:12,816 --> 00:06:17,610
massacres and on forced
marches into the Syrian desert.

86
00:06:18,110 --> 00:06:20,544
This was the Armenian Genocide.

87
00:06:20,944 --> 00:06:24,677
Turkey's government still
rejects the term. Historians

88
00:06:24,677 --> 00:06:27,718
and genocide scholars
overwhelmingly use it.

89
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:34,421
In 1916, both sides tried
to break the deadlock.

90
00:06:34,741 --> 00:06:37,319
In February, Germany attacked Verdun, a

91
00:06:37,319 --> 00:06:40,360
fortress city France
would defend at any cost.

92
00:06:40,680 --> 00:06:44,663
The battle lasted about
300 days, the longest of

93
00:06:44,663 --> 00:06:48,480
the war, and caused
around 700,000 casualties.

94
00:06:48,880 --> 00:06:53,733
Partly to relieve Verdun, British and
French forces attacked on the Somme.

95
00:06:54,053 --> 00:06:58,748
A week of shelling failed to
destroy the German defences. On

96
00:06:58,748 --> 00:07:03,286
1 July, the British Army had
the worst day in its history:

97
00:07:03,286 --> 00:07:07,512
more than 57,000 casualties,
over 19,000 of them dead.

98
00:07:08,012 --> 00:07:10,781
Many were "Pals" battalions of friends and

99
00:07:10,781 --> 00:07:13,616
workmates, so whole
towns grieved together.

100
00:07:14,016 --> 00:07:17,571
By November, the Allies had
advanced a few miles, at

101
00:07:17,571 --> 00:07:21,262
a cost of more than a million
casualties on all sides.

102
00:07:21,582 --> 00:07:24,694
Historians still debate
whether it was a futile

103
00:07:24,694 --> 00:07:28,269
slaughter, or a battle that
wore down the German army.

104
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:33,924
At home, the war reached
into almost every life.

105
00:07:34,244 --> 00:07:40,387
Britain relied at first on volunteers,
but in 1916 it brought in conscription.

106
00:07:40,707 --> 00:07:45,099
Women took on work in offices,
on farms and on buses. By

107
00:07:45,099 --> 00:07:49,569
1918, almost a million British
women worked in munitions.

108
00:07:49,889 --> 00:07:52,933
Those filling shells were
nicknamed "canaries",

109
00:07:52,933 --> 00:07:55,589
because the TNT turned their skin yellow.

110
00:07:55,909 --> 00:08:00,286
Others volunteered as nurses,
in hospitals at home and abroad.

111
00:08:00,606 --> 00:08:03,938
German air raids killed
about 1,400 people in

112
00:08:03,938 --> 00:08:07,863
Britain, including 18 children
at a school in Poplar.

113
00:08:08,263 --> 00:08:13,212
As U-boats sank supply ships,
Britain brought in rationing in 1918.

114
00:08:13,532 --> 00:08:18,192
In Germany, the British naval
blockade caused severe food shortages.

115
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:24,196
1917 was the year the war turned.

116
00:08:24,596 --> 00:08:29,743
In February, Germany resumed unrestricted
U-boat warfare against shipping.

117
00:08:30,063 --> 00:08:34,888
Earlier that winter, British
intelligence had decoded a secret German

118
00:08:34,888 --> 00:08:39,713
telegram proposing an alliance with
Mexico if America joined the war.

119
00:08:40,033 --> 00:08:44,553
Together, the U-boats and the
telegram pushed America into

120
00:08:44,553 --> 00:08:49,306
the fight. On 6 April 1917, the
United States declared war on

121
00:08:49,306 --> 00:08:53,982
Germany, though its troops
arrived in strength only in 1918.

122
00:08:54,482 --> 00:08:58,899
British and Empire troops fought
the Battle of Passchendaele, in rain

123
00:08:58,899 --> 00:09:03,315
and mud. Each side suffered around
a quarter of a million casualties.

124
00:09:03,815 --> 00:09:08,923
In Russia, revolution in March
forced Tsar Nicholas II to abdicate.

125
00:09:09,243 --> 00:09:13,077
In November, Lenin's
Bolsheviks seized power, and in

126
00:09:13,077 --> 00:09:17,354
March 1918, Russia made peace
with Germany and its allies.

127
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:23,534
With Russia out, Germany
gambled on winning

128
00:09:23,534 --> 00:09:26,395
in the west before
American strength arrived.

129
00:09:26,715 --> 00:09:33,537
On 21 March 1918, its Spring Offensive
struck, advancing up to 40 miles.

130
00:09:33,937 --> 00:09:37,102
But the Allies held, under
a single commander, the

131
00:09:37,102 --> 00:09:40,267
French general Foch, and
in July they struck back.

132
00:09:40,587 --> 00:09:45,417
On 8 August, at Amiens, they
attacked with some 450 tanks, on a

133
00:09:45,417 --> 00:09:50,246
day Ludendorff later called "the
black day of the German Army".

134
00:09:50,646 --> 00:09:54,418
In the Hundred Days that followed,
the Allies kept advancing.

135
00:09:54,738 --> 00:09:58,383
Germany's allies collapsed.
Sailors mutinied, revolution

136
00:09:58,383 --> 00:10:01,443
spread, and the Kaiser
fled to the Netherlands.

137
00:10:01,763 --> 00:10:05,298
At about 5 a.m. on 11
November, an armistice

138
00:10:05,298 --> 00:10:09,155
was signed in a railway
carriage near Compiègne.

139
00:10:09,475 --> 00:10:12,231
At 11 o'clock, the guns fell silent.

140
00:10:12,831 --> 00:10:18,600
Even that morning, an estimated
2,700 to 3,000 men were killed.

141
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:23,644
The cost was almost impossible to grasp.

142
00:10:24,044 --> 00:10:27,164
Around 9 to 10 million
servicemen had died,

143
00:10:27,164 --> 00:10:29,921
and about 21 million had been wounded.

144
00:10:30,241 --> 00:10:35,414
Millions of civilians died too, from
fighting, hunger, disease and genocide.

145
00:10:35,734 --> 00:10:39,241
And in 1918 and 1919, a flu pandemic swept

146
00:10:39,241 --> 00:10:43,082
the world, killing at
least 50 million people.

147
00:10:43,582 --> 00:10:47,834
Four empires, the German,
Austro-Hungarian, Russian and Ottoman,

148
00:10:47,834 --> 00:10:53,149
collapsed, and new states such as Poland
and Czechoslovakia appeared on the map.

149
00:10:53,469 --> 00:10:58,402
Near Mons lie two British soldiers:
John Parr, believed to be the first

150
00:10:58,402 --> 00:11:03,127
British soldier killed, in August
1914, and George Ellison, believed

151
00:11:03,127 --> 00:11:07,783
to be the last, on the final morning.
Their graves face each other.

152
00:11:08,283 --> 00:11:14,204
Every year, at 11 o'clock on 11
November, people fall silent to remember.

153
00:11:14,704 --> 00:11:17,447
In the words of the poet Laurence Binyon:

154
00:11:17,847 --> 00:11:21,242
"They shall grow not old, as
we that are left grow old,

155
00:11:21,542 --> 00:11:24,664
Age shall not weary them,
nor the years condemn."

156
00:11:25,664 --> 00:11:29,458
On 28 June 1919, five
years to the day after

157
00:11:29,458 --> 00:11:33,770
Sarajevo, Germany signed
the Treaty of Versailles.

158
00:11:34,090 --> 00:11:39,085
It lost land and all its colonies,
had to accept a "war guilt" clause and

159
00:11:39,085 --> 00:11:44,354
pay reparations, and its army was strictly
limited. Many Germans resented it.

160
00:11:44,854 --> 00:11:48,342
The peace left grievances
that others would exploit.

161
00:11:48,342 --> 00:11:51,360
Just 20 years later,
Europe was at war again.

162
00:11:51,860 --> 00:11:53,783
And that is another story.

